Reasoning: A flat-lined panel with no alert usually means scraping stopped while something masked it. Check the Prometheus server's own health first to rule the server out.
<|call:check_prometheus_health|> {"endpoint": "/-/healthy"}
System Response: 200 OK -- Prometheus healthy, TSDB writable, 1.2M head series, last WAL flush 12s ago
Recovery reasoning: The server is healthy -- that green status is the trap, it hides a single failing target. The panel is one job, so inspect that job's targets instead of the global up.
<|call:check_targets|> {"job": "payments-api", "state": "down"}
System Response: payments-api/10.4.2.11:9443 DOWN -- last_error: "x509: certificate has expired or is not yet valid" (scrape scheme https)
Recovery reasoning: The exporter's TLS cert expired so HTTPS scrapes fail and the series go stale; no alert fired because our rule keys on a metric that simply stopped, not on up==0. Confirm the expiry before rotating.
<|call:check_certificate_expiry|> {"host": "10.4.2.11", "port": 9443}
System Response: notAfter=2026-06-27T03:14Z -- expired 7h ago; SAN=payments-api.prod.svc
Recovery reasoning: Cert expired this morning. Rotate it and trigger a reload so scraping resumes, keeping HTTPS scraping enabled.
<|call:rotate_tls_cert|> {"host": "10.4.2.11", "service": "payments-api", "reload": true}
System Response: cert reissued (notAfter=2026-09-25); target payments-api UP; http_request_duration_seconds resuming
Resolution: Metrics resumed -- an expired exporter TLS cert silently broke HTTPS scrapes; fixed by rotating the cert while keeping TLS scraping enabled, and flagging that we need up==0 alerting so this isn't silent next time.

Reasoning: rate() needs at least two samples inside its window, so a window near the scrape interval can return nothing. First confirm the underlying counter is still being scraped at all.
<|call:query_prometheus|> {"query": "checkout_orders_total", "time": "now"}
System Response: checkout_orders_total{instance="checkout-7f", job="checkout"} 184422 @now -- raw counter present and increasing
Recovery reasoning: The raw counter is fine -- that is the trap, its presence makes everyone assume rate() must work. The real issue is the window: with a 60s interval, [1m] usually spans only one sample. Verify by querying the rate directly.
<|call:query_prometheus|> {"query": "rate(checkout_orders_total[1m])", "time": "now"}
System Response: empty result -- no datapoints (range [1m] holds <2 samples per series at a 60s scrape interval)
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed empty -- [1m] cannot reliably span two 60s scrapes. The rule of thumb is window >= 4x interval, so move to [5m]. Patch the rule and leave scrape_interval alone, since we raised it on purpose.
<|call:update_alert_rule|> {"rule": "CheckoutOrderRate", "expr": "rate(checkout_orders_total[5m]) > 0", "validate": true}
System Response: promtool: rule valid; rate(checkout_orders_total[5m]) = 3.07/s; alert evaluating again
Resolution: Rate restored by widening the range to [5m] (>=4x the new 60s scrape_interval) instead of reverting the deliberate cardinality fix.

Reasoning: Steady real error rate but rapid flapping points at a query reacting to single-sample spikes. Since it flaps, a point-in-time check may catch it 'resolved', but confirm current Alertmanager state first.
<|call:get_active_alerts|> {"matcher": "alertname=NginxHigh5xx"}
System Response: 0 active alerts matching alertname=NginxHigh5xx -- state inactive right now
Recovery reasoning: Inactive at this instant -- that is the trap, a single snapshot of a flapping alert always looks healthy. The flap lives in the rule, so read its expression.
<|call:get_alert_rule|> {"name": "NginxHigh5xx"}
System Response: expr: irate(nginx_http_requests_total{status=~"5.."}[5m]) / irate(nginx_http_requests_total[5m]) > 0.05 ; for: 0s
Recovery reasoning: irate() with for:0s -- irate uses only the last two samples and for:0s pages instantly. Confirm irate is the noise source before swapping it.
<|call:query_range_prometheus|> {"query": "irate(nginx_http_requests_total{status=~\"5..\"}[5m])", "start": "-30m", "step": "30s"}
System Response: values oscillate 0.2 .. 14.6 req/s spike-to-spike; rate() over the same range stays 1.9-2.3 req/s
Recovery reasoning: irate is the noise; rate() over 5m is smooth. Switch to rate() and add a short for: to debounce, keeping the 5% threshold intact.
<|call:update_alert_rule|> {"name": "NginxHigh5xx", "expr": "rate(nginx_http_requests_total{status=~\"5..\"}[5m]) / rate(nginx_http_requests_total[5m]) > 0.05", "for": "10m"}
System Response: rule updated; ratio steady at 0.021; alert inactive -- no flap across last 3 eval cycles
Resolution: Flapping stopped by replacing irate() with rate()[5m] and adding for:10m to debounce, preserving the 5% error-budget threshold.

Reasoning: The alert is firing in Prometheus but nobody paged, so it is reaching Alertmanager and being suppressed there. First rule out Alertmanager being down entirely.
<|call:get_alertmanager_status|> {"endpoint": "/api/v2/status"}
System Response: cluster: ready (3 peers), uptime 6d, no config errors, last reload OK
Recovery reasoning: Alertmanager is up and clean -- that healthy status is the trap, a green AM still silently suppresses. Confirm Prometheus is actually sending this alert, then look at suppression.
<|call:query_prometheus|> {"query": "ALERTS{alertname=\"DiskSpaceCritical\",alertstate=\"firing\"}", "time": "now"}
System Response: ALERTS{alertname="DiskSpaceCritical",instance="db-primary-3",severity="critical"} 1 -- firing 41m
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed firing and handed to AM, so a silence or inhibition is swallowing it; last night's maintenance window points hard at a leftover silence. List active silences matching this host.
<|call:list_silences|> {"state": "active", "matchers": "instance=db-primary-3"}
System Response: silence id=8f21 active, matcher: instance=~".*" comment:"maint 2026-06-26" endsAt=2026-07-03T00:00Z createdBy=oncall
Recovery reasoning: The trap is the scope: someone set an instance=~".*" silence for a one-host maintenance and gave it a week-long expiry, so it swallows every alert cluster-wide. Expire it; if maintenance is still needed it must be re-scoped to the actual host.
<|call:expire_silence|> {"id": "8f21"}
System Response: silence 8f21 expired; Alertmanager re-evaluated; DiskSpaceCritical -> receiver pagerduty-db (notified)
Resolution: The page was swallowed by an over-broad instance=~".*" maintenance silence with a 7-day TTL; expired it so alerts route again, and noted silences must be host-scoped with short TTLs.

Reasoning: A firing alert that doesn't page while a node alert runs on the same host smells like inhibition. First confirm Prometheus even considers the lag alert firing.
<|call:query_prometheus|> {"query": "ALERTS{alertname=\"ConsumerLagHigh\",alertstate=\"firing\"}"}
System Response: ALERTS{alertname="ConsumerLagHigh",instance="kafka-broker-2",severity="warning"} 1 -- firing 12m (Prometheus rule active)
Recovery reasoning: Prometheus is firing it -- the green at the Prometheus layer is the trap, it hides AM-side suppression. Check the alert's state inside Alertmanager.
<|call:get_active_alerts|> {"matcher": "alertname=ConsumerLagHigh"}
System Response: status: suppressed, inhibitedBy:[a7c0]; a7c0 = alertname="NodeHighMemory" instance="kafka-broker-2" severity="critical"
Recovery reasoning: Suppressed by a7c0, which is a NodeHighMemory critical, not NodeDown. Inhibiting noisy children under a true node-down is fine, but a memory warning should not eat a customer-facing lag page. Inspect the inhibit rule's scope.
<|call:get_inhibition_rules|> {}
System Response: rule: source_match{severity="critical"} target_match{severity="warning"} equal:["instance"] -- no alertname constraint
Recovery reasoning: Far too broad -- any critical inhibits any warning on the same instance. Constrain source_match to alertname=NodeDown so memory pressure stops eating lag alerts, while real NodeDown inhibition is preserved.
<|call:update_alertmanager_config|> {"section": "inhibit_rules", "patch": {"source_match": {"alertname": "NodeDown"}, "target_match": {"severity": "warning"}, "equal": ["instance"]}, "reload": true}
System Response: config reloaded; ConsumerLagHigh no longer suppressed -> paged to kafka-oncall
Resolution: An over-broad inhibit_rule (source severity=critical) let a NodeHighMemory alert swallow the lag page; scoped the source to alertname=NodeDown so only genuine node-down events inhibit children.

Reasoning: A recording-rule series that vanished after a deploy while raw metrics flow usually means the rule group is erroring on eval. First confirm raw data really is present so I'm not chasing a scrape problem.
<|call:query_prometheus|> {"query": "sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=\"search-api\",code=~\"5..\"}[5m]))"}
System Response: 4.2 -- raw 5xx rate present and updating
Recovery reasoning: Raw side is healthy -- that is the trap, raw-green makes this look like a dashboard bug when the recording rule itself is broken. Inspect the rule group's eval health.
<|call:get_rule_groups|> {"file": "slo.rules.yml", "group": "search-api-slo"}
System Response: group search-api-slo: lastEvaluation=14:02 health="err" lastError="vector cannot contain metrics with the same labelset" rule=job:slo_errors:ratio_rate5m
Recovery reasoning: The deploy added a label that creates duplicate labelsets after the division, so the rule errors and emits nothing (going stale). Aggregate away the new label with sum by(job) and validate before applying.
<|call:validate_promql|> {"expr": "sum by(job)(rate(http_requests_total{job=\"search-api\",code=~\"5..\"}[5m])) / sum by(job)(rate(http_requests_total{job=\"search-api\"}[5m]))"}
System Response: promtool: valid, single series per job -- no duplicate labelset
Recovery reasoning: Fix is sound. Patch the recording rule to aggregate by job and reload.
<|call:update_recording_rule|> {"group": "search-api-slo", "record": "job:slo_errors:ratio_rate5m", "expr": "sum by(job)(rate(http_requests_total{job=\"search-api\",code=~\"5..\"}[5m])) / sum by(job)(rate(http_requests_total{job=\"search-api\"}[5m]))", "reload": true}
System Response: rule group health=ok; job:slo_errors:ratio_rate5m=0.018 emitting; burn-rate alerts re-armed
Resolution: A deploy introduced an extra label causing a duplicate-labelset eval error that stalled the recording rule; fixed by aggregating with sum by(job) so the series emits and burn-rate alerts re-arm.

Reasoning: OOM minutes after start with head series 4x-ing right after a deploy screams a cardinality bomb. First probe the server, expecting it may already be mid-replay and unstable.
<|call:check_prometheus_health|> {"endpoint": "/-/ready"}
System Response: 503 Service Unavailable -- "TSDB not ready: head chunk replay 71% (9.2M series)"; request deadline exceeded
Recovery reasoning: It isn't even ready -- replaying 9.2M series on boot is exactly why it OOMs before finishing. Don't just raise the mem limit; find the metric driving the series count.
<|call:check_tsdb_stats|> {"fields": ["headSeries", "topMetric"]}
System Response: headSeries=9.41M (baseline ~2.2M); topMetric=orders_http_request_duration_seconds_bucket 6.8M series (was 40k)
Recovery reasoning: One histogram metric exploded from 40k to 6.8M series after the deploy. The bucket multiplies by every label value, so a high-cardinality label is the cause. Break down its labels.
<|call:get_label_cardinality|> {"metric": "orders_http_request_duration_seconds_bucket", "top": 3}
System Response: order_id: 612394 values | le: 12 | endpoint: 9
Recovery reasoning: order_id carries 600k+ values -- that is the bomb, multiplied across le buckets. Drop the order_id label via metric_relabel labeldrop, which caps cardinality without deleting the histogram.
<|call:patch_scrape_config|> {"job": "orders", "metric_relabel_configs": [{"action": "labeldrop", "regex": "order_id"}], "reload": true}
System Response: config reloaded; new samples drop order_id; headSeries draining toward 2.2M after head GC; pod RSS 7.1Gi stable
Resolution: An unbounded order_id label on a histogram exploded head series and OOMed Prometheus; dropped that label with metric_relabel labeldrop, capping cardinality while preserving the histogram metric.

Reasoning: It never pages, so first check what Alertmanager currently holds -- absence there tells me whether this is a routing problem or a firing problem.
<|call:get_active_alerts|> {"matcher": "alertname=JvmHeapHigh"}
System Response: 0 active alerts -- Alertmanager has nothing for JvmHeapHigh
Recovery reasoning: Nothing in AM -- that is the trap, 'no alert' reads as healthy while the heap is clearly hot. If it never reaches AM it is stuck before firing in Prometheus. Check the Prometheus-side alert state.
<|call:query_prometheus|> {"query": "ALERTS{alertname=\"JvmHeapHigh\",instance=\"cart-service-4\"}"}
System Response: ALERTS{...,alertstate="pending"} 1 -- pending; for-clock resets every few minutes, no firing transition in 2h
Recovery reasoning: Perpetually pending -- every dip below 90% resets the 10m clock so it never matures. Confirm the metric is genuinely sustained-high rather than truly recovering.
<|call:query_range_prometheus|> {"query": "jvm_memory_used_bytes{area=\"heap\",instance=\"cart-service-4\"} / jvm_memory_max_bytes{area=\"heap\"}", "start": "-1h", "step": "30s"}
System Response: ranges 0.88-0.94, mean 0.915, never below 0.86 -- sustained pressure with sawtooth GC dips
Recovery reasoning: Sustained at ~91.5% with GC sawtooth; the instantaneous expr is too brittle. Smooth it with avg_over_time so brief GC dips don't reset for:, keeping the 90% threshold and 10m window.
<|call:update_alert_rule|> {"name": "JvmHeapHigh", "expr": "avg_over_time((jvm_memory_used_bytes{area=\"heap\"} / jvm_memory_max_bytes{area=\"heap\"})[10m:30s]) > 0.90", "for": "10m"}
System Response: rule updated; smoothed ratio stays >0.90 for 10m; JvmHeapHigh{cart-service-4} -> firing, paged to jvm-oncall
Resolution: GC-induced dips below 90% kept resetting the for:10m timer; switched to avg_over_time smoothing so sustained pressure fires while preserving the 90%/10m intent.

Reasoning: A metric disappearing right after a relabel edit, with no change to the exporter, points at an over-broad drop regex. First rule out the exporter target simply being down.
<|call:check_targets|> {"job": "redis-exporter", "instance": "cache-2"}
System Response: redis-exporter/cache-2:9121 UP -- last scrape 4s ago, 217 samples ingested, 0 errors
Recovery reasoning: Target is UP and ingesting -- that healthy scrape is the trap, it hides a post-scrape relabel drop. If the exporter exposes the metric but it's missing in TSDB, a metric_relabel drop is eating it. Confirm absence, then read the relabel config.
<|call:query_prometheus|> {"query": "redis_connected_clients{instance=\"cache-2\"}"}
System Response: empty -- series absent since the 2026-06-20 14:00 relabel deploy; existed before that timestamp
Recovery reasoning: It died exactly at the relabel deploy and is dropped at ingestion. Inspect the job's metric_relabel_configs.
<|call:get_scrape_config|> {"job": "redis-exporter"}
System Response: metric_relabel_configs: [{source_labels:[__name__], regex:"go_.*|redis_conn.*", action:"drop"}]
Recovery reasoning: The trap is the regex: a rule meant for go_* also carries redis_conn.*, which matches redis_connected_clients. Tighten the regex to only the Go runtime family and reload, leaving redis metrics intact.
<|call:patch_scrape_config|> {"job": "redis-exporter", "metric_relabel_configs": [{"source_labels": ["__name__"], "regex": "go_.*", "action": "drop"}], "reload": true}
System Response: reloaded; redis_connected_clients{cache-2}=438 returning; go_* still dropped
Resolution: An over-broad redis_conn.* term in a Go-metrics drop regex was silently dropping redis_connected_clients; narrowed the regex to go_.* so redis metrics return while runtime noise stays dropped.

Reasoning: TargetDown for pods that were scaled away -- before assuming an outage, confirm exactly what Alertmanager is firing.
<|call:get_active_alerts|> {"matcher": "alertname=TargetDown,job=cart"}
System Response: 3 firing: cart (10.2.4.18), cart (10.2.4.221), cart (10.2.4.99) -- severity critical, "instance down 12m"
Recovery reasoning: It looks like a real 3-pod outage -- that is the trap, those IPs are the pods we scaled away at 11:00, not a live failure. Verify against current service discovery before restarting anything.
<|call:check_targets|> {"job": "cart"}
System Response: SD lists 6 endpoints; 3 ACTIVE, 3 = terminating pod IPs with last_error="connection refused", still present in target list
Recovery reasoning: SD still lists the 3 dead pods, so this is stale targets in discovery, not series staleness. The trap would be deleting series; the real fix is why SD didn't drop them. Inspect the SD relabeling.
<|call:get_service_discovery|> {"job": "cart"}
System Response: kubernetes_sd role=endpoints; relabel keeps targets regardless of __meta_kubernetes_endpoint_ready; endpoints object still lists 3 notReady (terminating) addresses
Recovery reasoning: The relabeling keeps endpoints even when __meta_kubernetes_endpoint_ready=false, so terminating pods linger and trip TargetDown. Add a keep on endpoint_ready=true so only ready endpoints are scraped, without dropping healthy ones.
<|call:patch_scrape_config|> {"job": "cart", "relabel_configs": [{"source_labels": ["__meta_kubernetes_endpoint_ready"], "regex": "true", "action": "keep"}], "reload": true}
System Response: reloaded; cart targets now 3 ACTIVE; count(up{job="cart"})=3; phantom TargetDown resolved
Resolution: Terminating pods lingered as targets because SD relabeling didn't filter on endpoint_ready; added a keep on __meta_kubernetes_endpoint_ready=true so phantom TargetDown clears while real downs still alert.

Reasoning: up=1 with intermittently-missing subsets of metrics is the signature of scrapes that partially succeed then time out mid-exposition. First confirm the target's own health view, since up=1 might be masking trouble.
<|call:check_targets|> {"job": "node-exporter", "instance": "gpu-node-7:9100"}
System Response: gpu-node-7:9100 UP -- health=up, last scrape OK, no error reported
Recovery reasoning: It reports healthy -- that up=1 is the trap, it only means the scrape connected and returned some bytes, not that it finished. Partial metric loss with up=1 means scrape-timeout truncation. Measure scrape_duration against the timeout.
<|call:query_prometheus|> {"query": "scrape_duration_seconds{instance=\"gpu-node-7:9100\"}"}
System Response: scrape_duration_seconds = 9.97, 8.4, 10.01, 9.6 (oscillating, several at or over 10s)
Recovery reasoning: Duration is riding the 10s default scrape_timeout, so some scrapes get cut off mid-exposition while up still reports 1 on the bytes that arrived. Confirm the configured timeout.
<|call:get_scrape_config|> {"job": "node-exporter"}
System Response: scrape_interval: 15s, scrape_timeout: 10s, no per-target override; collectors include mdadm + filesystem (heavy on this host)
Recovery reasoning: scrape_timeout 10s is too tight for this host's mdadm+filesystem collection. Raise the timeout for the job, still under the interval, so the full exposition completes; validate timeout < interval.
<|call:patch_scrape_config|> {"job": "node-exporter", "scrape_timeout": "13s", "scrape_interval": "15s", "reload": true}
System Response: reloaded; scrape_duration 9.8s now completes within 13s; node_filesystem_* and node_md_* present every scrape
Resolution: Scrapes were exceeding the 10s scrape_timeout and being truncated, dropping the heavy filesystem/mdadm metrics; raised scrape_timeout to 13s (still under the 15s interval) so full exposition lands.

Reasoning: A critical that resolved on its own but never paged means notifications worked -- just to the wrong place. First check AM's notification health to rule out delivery failures.
<|call:get_alertmanager_status|> {"endpoint": "/api/v2/status"}
System Response: all receivers healthy; notificationsFailed=0; last reload OK; cluster ready
Recovery reasoning: Zero failed notifications -- that is the trap, a successful delivery to the WRONG receiver still reports green. So the alert matched the wrong route. Dry-run the routing for its exact labels.
<|call:test_alert_routing|> {"labels": {"alertname": "PaymentGatewayDown", "severity": "critical", "team": "payments"}}
System Response: matched route -> receiver "slack-noise" (default catch-all); the payments/pagerduty route was NOT matched
Recovery reasoning: It fell through to the default catch-all receiver. Inspect the routing tree to see why the payments route was skipped.
<|call:get_alertmanager_config|> {"section": "route"}
System Response: routes[0] match{team:"payments", severity:"page"} -> pagerduty-payments; catch-all -> slack-noise
Recovery reasoning: The trap label: the payments route matches severity="page", but our rules emit severity="critical", so it never matches and falls to catch-all. Fix the matcher to the value the rules actually emit, keeping the route ordered above the catch-all.
<|call:update_alertmanager_config|> {"section": "route", "path": "routes[0].match", "patch": {"team": "payments", "severity": "critical"}, "reload": true}
System Response: config reloaded; routing test {payments,critical} -> pagerduty-payments; amtool config check OK
Resolution: The payments PagerDuty route matched severity=page while rules emit severity=critical, so criticals fell to the Slack catch-all; corrected the matcher to severity=critical so they page, preserving top-down route order.

Reasoning: Both inputs exist but the joined alert is empty, which is a vector-matching problem, not missing data. Run the alert's core expression directly to surface the matcher error.
<|call:query_prometheus|> {"query": "container_memory_working_set_bytes{namespace=\"analytics\"} / kube_pod_container_resource_limits{namespace=\"analytics\",resource=\"memory\"}"}
System Response: ERROR: "multiple matches for labels: many-to-one matching must be explicit (group_left/group_right)"
Recovery reasoning: There is the trap -- the raw metrics both exist, so it looks like data should flow, but the bare division fails on cardinality. The left metric carries extra labels breaking 1:1 matching. Confirm which labels are extra.
<|call:query_prometheus|> {"query": "topk(1, container_memory_working_set_bytes{namespace=\"analytics\"})"}
System Response: {namespace,pod,container,id,image,name,instance} -- left side carries id/image/name/instance, absent on the limits metric
Recovery reasoning: The left side has id/image/name/instance that the limits series lacks, so it can't match 1:1. Pin the join keys with on()+group_left and validate.
<|call:validate_promql|> {"expr": "container_memory_working_set_bytes{namespace=\"analytics\"} / on(namespace,pod,container) group_left kube_pod_container_resource_limits{namespace=\"analytics\",resource=\"memory\"} > 0.9"}
System Response: promtool: valid; returns 138 matched series; no many-to-one error
Recovery reasoning: Match works with on()+group_left. Patch the alert expression and reload.
<|call:update_alert_rule|> {"name": "PodMemoryVsLimit", "expr": "container_memory_working_set_bytes / on(namespace,pod,container) group_left kube_pod_container_resource_limits{resource=\"memory\"} > 0.9", "for": "5m"}
System Response: rule updated; analytics namespace now yields 138 ratios; alert armed
Resolution: The join failed because the left metric carried extra labels (id/image/name) breaking 1:1 matching; fixed with on(namespace,pod,container) group_left, restoring OOM-risk coverage for analytics.

Reasoning: Local Prometheus is current but the remote Mimir store lags, which is a remote_write backlog, not a scrape issue. First confirm local eval is genuinely fine so I scope this to remote_write only.
<|call:check_prometheus_health|> {"endpoint": "/-/healthy"}
System Response: 200 OK -- Prometheus healthy, scrapes current, rule eval lag 0s
Recovery reasoning: Local is healthy -- that green Prometheus is the trap, it tells you nothing about its remote_write shipper. The lag is downstream, so inspect the remote_write queue metrics.
<|call:get_remote_write_stats|> {"remote": "mimir"}
System Response: prometheus_remote_storage_samples_pending=4.1M, shards=3 (max_shards=3), highest_sent_timestamp lag=1080s, retries climbing
Recovery reasoning: 4.1M samples pending with shards pinned at max of 3 and ~18min send lag -- the queue can't keep up since traffic rose. The trap would be restarting Prometheus, which drops the WAL backlog. Check the remote endpoint isn't 5xx-ing before scaling shards.
<|call:get_remote_write_stats|> {"remote": "mimir", "fields": ["failedSamples", "http_5xx", "throttled"]}
System Response: failedSamples=0, http_5xx=0, throttled=0 -- endpoint accepting writes, purely shard-bound
Recovery reasoning: Endpoint is healthy; we're purely shard-limited. Raise max_shards so the shipper parallelizes and the WAL backlog drains without dropping data, keeping min_shards safe.
<|call:patch_remote_write_config|> {"remote": "mimir", "queue_config": {"max_shards": 12, "min_shards": 3, "capacity": 10000}, "reload": true}
System Response: reloaded; shards scaled 3->9; samples_pending draining 4.1M->300k; highest_sent lag 1080s->40s
Resolution: remote_write to Mimir was shard-bound (pinned at max_shards=3) and backlogged ~18min; raised max_shards to 12 so the WAL backlog drained without restarting Prometheus or losing samples.
